Kept the standard DFS values here. `tin` stores when a vertex is first visited, and `low` stores the earliest discovery time reachable through tree edges and back edges. A non-root vertex `u` is an articulation point when one child `v` cannot reach above `u`.

The root is a little different: it needs at least two DFS children. The input graph is not changed.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> articulationPoints(int n, vector<int> adj[]) {
        vector<int> tin(n, -1), low(n, -1);
        vector<bool> cut(n, false);
        int timer = 0;

        function<void(int, int)> dfs = [&](int u, int parent) {
            tin[u] = low[u] = timer++;
            int children = 0;

            for (int v : adj[u]) {
                if (v == parent) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (tin[v] != -1) {
                    low[u] = min(low[u], tin[v]);
                } else {
                    dfs(v, u);
                    low[u] = min(low[u], low[v]);

                    if (parent != -1 && low[v] >= tin[u]) {
                        cut[u] = true;
                    }
                    ++children;
                }
            }

            if (parent == -1 && children > 1) {
                cut[u] = true;
            }
        };

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (tin[i] == -1) {
                dfs(i, -1);
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (cut[i]) {
                answer.push_back(i);
            }
        }

        if (answer.empty()) {
            answer.push_back(-1);
        }
        return answer;
    }
};
```

For a small chain `0 - 1 - 2`, vertex `1` gets a child whose `low` value cannot reach vertex `0`, so it is marked. The two endpoints are not marked.

Time complexity: `O(n + e)`, where `e` is the number of edges. Space complexity: `O(n)` besides the graph and the recursion stack.

A vertex can separate a child subtree only when that subtree has no back edge reaching an ancestor of the vertex. The `low` array records this reach, while `tin` records the DFS order.

The root needs separate handling. It is an articulation point only when DFS starts at two or more of its children. This code supports disconnected graphs and leaves the adjacency lists untouched.

```go
package main

func articulationPoints(n int, adj [][]int) []int {
	tin := make([]int, n)
	low := make([]int, n)
	isCut := make([]bool, n)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		tin[i] = -1
	}

	timer := 0
	var dfs func(int, int)
	dfs = func(node, parent int) {
		tin[node] = timer
		low[node] = timer
		timer++
		children := 0

		for _, next := range adj[node] {
			if next == parent {
				continue
			}

			if tin[next] != -1 {
				if tin[next] < low[node] {
					low[node] = tin[next]
				}
				continue
			}

			dfs(next, node)
			if low[next] < low[node] {
				low[node] = low[next]
			}

			if parent != -1 && low[next] >= tin[node] {
				isCut[node] = true
			}
			children++
		}

		if parent == -1 && children > 1 {
			isCut[node] = true
		}
	}

	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		if tin[i] == -1 {
			dfs(i, -1)
		}
	}

	answer := make([]int, 0)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		if isCut[i] {
			answer = append(answer, i)
		}
	}
	if len(answer) == 0 {
		return []int{-1}
	}
	return answer
}
```

In a star centered at `0`, the center gets several DFS children, so the root rule marks it. Each leaf has no child subtree and stays unmarked.

A single vertex or a graph with no separating vertex returns `[-1]`, which is the usual platform format.

Time complexity: `O(n + e)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the arrays and recursion stack.

This uses the edge-stack version of Tarjan's method. During DFS, edges are stored until a child subtree cannot connect to an earlier vertex. Those edges form one biconnected group, and every vertex in more than one group is a cut vertex.

A `HashSet` is used while popping one group so a vertex is counted once for that group. That makes the code a little longer, but it avoids special cases for repeated appearances inside the same group.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;

class Solution {
    private int timer;
    private int[] tin;
    private int[] low;
    private int[] groupCount;
    private ArrayDeque<int[]> edgeStack;
    private ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> graph;

    public ArrayList<Integer> articulationPoints(int n,
            ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {
        graph = adj;
        tin = new int[n];
        low = new int[n];
        groupCount = new int[n];
        edgeStack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            tin[i] = -1;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (tin[i] == -1) {
                dfs(i, -1);
            }
        }

        ArrayList<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (groupCount[i] > 1) {
                answer.add(i);
            }
        }
        if (answer.isEmpty()) {
            answer.add(-1);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private void dfs(int node, int parent) {
        tin[node] = low[node] = timer++;

        for (int next : graph.get(node)) {
            if (next == parent) {
                continue;
            }

            if (tin[next] == -1) {
                edgeStack.push(new int[] {node, next});
                dfs(next, node);
                low[node] = Math.min(low[node], low[next]);

                if (low[next] >= tin[node]) {
                    collectGroup(node, next);
                }
            } else if (tin[next] < tin[node]) {
                edgeStack.push(new int[] {node, next});
                low[node] = Math.min(low[node], tin[next]);
            }
        }
    }

    private void collectGroup(int start, int end) {
        HashSet<Integer> vertices = new HashSet<>();
        while (true) {
            int[] edge = edgeStack.pop();
            vertices.add(edge[0]);
            vertices.add(edge[1]);
            if ((edge[0] == start && edge[1] == end)
                    || (edge[0] == end && edge[1] == start)) {
                break;
            }
        }

        for (int vertex : vertices) {
            groupCount[vertex]++;
        }
    }
}
```

For a triangle, all three edges are popped as one group, so every vertex has a group count of one. The result is `[-1]`. If two dense parts share one vertex, that shared vertex belongs to two groups and is reported.

Time complexity: `O(n + e)` for a simple undirected graph. Space complexity: `O(n + e)` for the DFS arrays, edge stack, and temporary vertex set.

Here the DFS keeps the edges of the current biconnected part on a stack. When `low[child] >= tin[node]`, that part is popped. A vertex appearing in more than one popped part is an articulation point.

This is a different way to mark the answer from checking the child condition directly. Isolated vertices have no edges to pop, so they are not incorrectly reported.

```python
class Solution:
    def articulationPoints(self, n, adj):
        discovery = [-1] * n
        low = [0] * n
        edge_stack = []
        part_count = [0] * n
        timer = 0

        def pop_component(stop_edge):
            vertices = set()
            while True:
                a, b = edge_stack.pop()
                vertices.add(a)
                vertices.add(b)
                if (a, b) == stop_edge or (b, a) == stop_edge:
                    break

            for vertex in vertices:
                part_count[vertex] += 1

        def dfs(node, parent):
            nonlocal timer
            discovery[node] = low[node] = timer
            timer += 1

            for neighbor in adj[node]:
                if neighbor == parent:
                    continue

                if discovery[neighbor] == -1:
                    edge_stack.append((node, neighbor))
                    dfs(neighbor, node)
                    low[node] = min(low[node], low[neighbor])

                    if low[neighbor] >= discovery[node]:
                        pop_component((node, neighbor))
                elif discovery[neighbor] < discovery[node]:
                    edge_stack.append((node, neighbor))
                    low[node] = min(low[node], discovery[neighbor])

        for start in range(n):
            if discovery[start] == -1:
                dfs(start, -1)

        answer = [vertex for vertex in range(n) if part_count[vertex] > 1]
        return answer if answer else [-1]
```

Dry run on `0 - 1 - 2`: the first popped part contains vertices `1` and `2`. The next one contains `0` and `1`. Vertex `1` appears in two parts, so it is the only answer.

The code does not modify `adj`. For very deep graphs, Python's recursion limit may need attention because the DFS is recursive.

Time complexity: `O(n + e)`, since each edge is pushed and popped a constant number of times. Space complexity: `O(n + e)` for DFS data and the edge stack.

This version checks the definition directly. First, it counts the connected components of the original graph. Then every vertex is skipped once, and the component count is calculated again. If the count becomes larger, that vertex is an articulation point.

It is slower than the usual low-link DFS, but the condition is easy to follow and it does not need discovery times. The adjacency lists are only read.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> articulationPoints(int n,
            ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {
        ArrayList<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        int originalComponents = countComponents(n, adj, -1);

        for (int removed = 0; removed < n; removed++) {
            int remainingComponents = countComponents(n, adj, removed);
            if (remainingComponents > originalComponents) {
                answer.add(removed);
            }
        }

        if (answer.isEmpty()) {
            answer.add(-1);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private int countComponents(int n, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj,
            int removed) {
        boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];
        if (removed != -1) {
            seen[removed] = true;
        }

        int components = 0;
        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            if (seen[start]) {
                continue;
            }

            components++;
            ArrayList<Integer> stack = new ArrayList<>();
            stack.add(start);
            seen[start] = true;

            while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                int u = stack.remove(stack.size() - 1);
                for (int v : adj.get(u)) {
                    if (!seen[v]) {
                        seen[v] = true;
                        stack.add(v);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return components;
    }
}
```

For a graph with one connected component, removing the middle vertex of a three-vertex chain creates two components. Removing an endpoint leaves one component, so it is not added.

This also handles a graph that starts with several components because it compares both counts instead of assuming the graph is connected.

Time complexity: `O(n * (n + e))`. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the visited array and stack.

